Subject: [PATCH 08009/11362] [outer-loop] Per-test attribution for batch 01:
 README.md is rewritten by a test that says so in its own name
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Docs only, no code change. The audit-hook plugin attributes 13 of batch 01's 16 moved files to an
exact test node.

README.md — CLAUDE.md's Public Documentation Discipline lists it as operator-curated and forbids the
autonomous loop from editing it — is rewritten by
tests/python/test_experiment_209_cleanup.py::test_run_cleanup_rewrites_public_docs_with_provenance_labels
(plus 3 siblings, and test_experiment_1931_hf_publisher.py::test_success_upload and
test_experiment_1750.py::{blocked,success}). A PASSING test does it on every suite run, and the
hazard commit's list of 39 did not include README.md at all. The same batch attributes
openspec/papers/paper-v6/section-6-limitations.md to test_experiment_1911.py::test_experiment_1911_schema.

INSTRUMENT LIMIT, RECORDED RATHER THAN PAPERED OVER. Three of the 16 are UNATTRIBUTED:
output/kanele_synth/post_synth.dcp, results/experiment_1822_rtl_synth.log, and
results/experiment_2510_ensemble_v7.json. A CPython audit hook only observes writes from the process
it is installed in, so a script that shells out to Vivado or yosys writes from a CHILD process it
never sees. Those files show up in git status (hence in the batch total) but cannot be attributed
per-test by this instrument. Subprocess writers are a separate class needing a different tool —
strace/fanotify, or wrapping subprocess.run — and anyone extending this survey should know that
before trusting an "unattributed" row.

